const title = "How to Play Shikaku — Beginner's Guide | GridPaw";
const description = "Learn Shikaku rules step by step with this beginner's guide. Understand the goal, master rectangle placement, and play free online at GridPaw.";

const faqs = [
  { q: "Is Shikaku the same as Rectangle Partition?", a: "Yes. Shikaku is the original Japanese name used by Nikoli. English-language puzzle sites often call it Rectangle Partition, Rectangles, or Divide by Box. The rules are identical: split the grid into rectangles so each one contains exactly one number and covers exactly that many cells." },
  { q: "How is Shikaku different from Sudoku?", a: "Sudoku asks you to fill digits into cells; Shikaku asks you to draw shapes around cells. In Sudoku every cell gets a number, while in Shikaku most cells are empty and the few given numbers define the area of the rectangle that must contain them. Both are pure logic puzzles with a single solution, but Shikaku is spatial rather than numeric." },
  { q: "Do I need to guess in Shikaku?", a: "No. Every well-constructed Shikaku puzzle has exactly one solution that can be reached by deduction alone. If you feel stuck, look for a number with only one legal rectangle shape, or find an empty cell that only one rectangle can reach. There is always a next logical step." },
  { q: "Can I play Shikaku on my phone?", a: "Yes. GridPaw runs in any modern mobile browser with no download required. Drag your finger across the grid to draw a rectangle, tap a rectangle to remove it, and use the Undo button to step back. Your level progress is saved automatically on your device." },
  { q: "How many levels does GridPaw have?", a: "GridPaw generates a fresh puzzle for every level, so there is no fixed cap. Levels 1 to 5 are 4×4 tutorials, and the grid grows through Easy, Medium, Hard, Expert, and Master tiers until you reach Legend at level 51 and beyond, where every board is a full 8×8 grid." },
];
